&ldquo;You will be found&mdash;by the passing public. They will be dropping along
      about three hours from now, and will spread the news&mdash;do you hear? Good-by. You have seen the last of
      me.&rdquo;

&ldquo;You will add no signature. He must receive this before he learns of the
      reward-placard&mdash;before he rises in the morning&mdash;lest he lose his
      head and fly the place penniless.&rdquo;

The boy thanked him with tears in his eyes, but shuddered and said he
      &ldquo;dasn&rsquo;t risk it&rdquo;; he said Flint would catch him alone, some time, in the
      night, and then&mdash;&ldquo;Oh, it makes me sick, Mr. Riley, to think of it.&rdquo;

&ldquo;It was an accident, you know. Don&rsquo;t say anything about it to anybody; I
      was excited, and didn&rsquo;t notice what I was doing. You&rsquo;re not looking well;
      you&rsquo;ve worked enough for to-day; go down to my cabin and eat what you
      want, and rest. It&rsquo;s just an accident, you know, on account of my being
      excited.&rdquo;

&ldquo;The regularest man that ever was,&rdquo; said Jake Parker, the blacksmith; &ldquo;you
      can tell when it&rsquo;s twelve just by him leaving, without looking at your
      Waterbury.&rdquo;

&ldquo;Nor anybody else,&rdquo; said Ham Sandwich; &ldquo;and if they are 15-puzzles how are
      you going to rank up that other one? When it comes to A 1 right-down solid
      mysteriousness, he lays over both of them. Easy&mdash;don&rsquo;t he?&rdquo;

Everybody said it. Every man but one. He was the new-comer&mdash;Peterson.
      He ordered the drinks all round, and asked who No. 3 might be. All
      answered at once, &ldquo;Archy Stillman!&rdquo;

&ldquo;Sit down, sit down, Mrs. Hogan, and don&rsquo;t worry. He asked for a bed three
      hours ago, tuckered out tramping the trails the way he&rsquo;s always doing, and
      went upstairs. Ham Sandwich, run up and roust him out; he&rsquo;s in No. 14.&rdquo;

&ldquo;Hand me a lantern,&rdquo; said Archy. He set it on the hard earth floor and
      knelt by it, pretending to examine the ground closely. &ldquo;Here&rsquo;s her track,&rdquo;
      he said, touching the ground here and there and yonder with his finger.
      &ldquo;Do you see?&rdquo;

Young Stillman stepped outside, held the light to the ground, turned
      leftward, and moved three steps, closely examining; then said, &ldquo;I&rsquo;ve got
      the direction&mdash;come along; take the lantern, somebody.&rdquo;

He took a lantern and examined the ground for a matter of twenty yards;
      then said, &ldquo;Come on; it&rsquo;s all right,&rdquo; and gave up the lantern. In and out
      among the sage-bushes he marched, a quarter of a mile, bearing gradually
      to the right; then took a new direction and made another great semicircle;
      then changed again and moved due west nearly half a mile&mdash;and
      stopped.

&ldquo;Wonderful performance to find this place, wasn&rsquo;t it? Hardly worth while
      to come so far, though; any other supposititious place would have answered
      just as well&mdash;hey?&rdquo;

&ldquo;Do you mean to insinuate that the child hasn&rsquo;t been here? I tell you the
      child has been here! Now if you want to get yourself into as tidy a little
      fuss as&mdash;&rdquo;

&ldquo;Nothing? Do you call that nothing?&rdquo; and he swiftly traced upon the ground
      a form with his finger. &ldquo;There&mdash;don&rsquo;t you recognize it now? It&rsquo;s
      Injun Billy&rsquo;s track. He&rsquo;s got the child.&rdquo;

&ldquo;I find her bymeby it is ten o&rsquo;clock,&rdquo; Billy explained. &ldquo;She &rsquo;sleep out
      yonder, ve&rsquo;y tired&mdash;face wet, been cryin&rsquo;, &rsquo;spose; fetch her home,
      feed her, she heap much hungry&mdash;go &rsquo;sleep &rsquo;gin.&rdquo;

&ldquo;And that paleness,&rdquo; said Ham Sandwich. &ldquo;Comes from thought&mdash;that&rsquo;s
      what it comes from. Hell! duffers like us don&rsquo;t know what real thought
      is.&rdquo;

&ldquo;Right you are, Wells-Fargo. And look at that frown&mdash;that&rsquo;s deep
      thinking&mdash;away down, down, forty fathom into the bowels of things.
      He&rsquo;s on the track of something.&rdquo;

&ldquo;Well, he is, and don&rsquo;t you forget it. Say&mdash;look at that awful
      gravity&mdash;look at that pallid solemness&mdash;there ain&rsquo;t any corpse
      can lay over it.&rdquo;

&ldquo;&rsquo;Sh! Watch him! There&mdash;he&rsquo;s got his thumb on the bump on the near
      corner of his forehead, and his forefinger on the off one. His think-works
      is just a-grinding now, you bet your other shirt.&rdquo;

&ldquo;Now he has rose up standing, and is putting his clues together on his
      left fingers with his right finger. See? he touches the forefinger&mdash;now
      middle finger&mdash;now ring-finger&mdash;&rdquo;

&ldquo;&lsquo;Um&mdash;young, weak&mdash;two miles. Weariness will overtake it then.
      It will sink down and sleep. We shall find it two miles away, or less.
      Teeth?&rsquo;

&ldquo;&lsquo;Very good, very good, very good, indeed.&rsquo; You see, boys, he knows a clue
      when he sees it, when it wouldn&rsquo;t mean a dern thing to anybody else.
      &lsquo;Stockings, madam? Shoes?&rsquo;

&ldquo;By Jackson, it&rsquo;s grand!&rdquo; said Ham Sandwich. &ldquo;Wells-Fargo, you&rsquo;ve got him
      down to a dot. He ain&rsquo;t painted up any exacter to the life in the books.
      By George, I can just see him&mdash;can&rsquo;t you, boys?&rdquo;

&ldquo;Uncle Sherlock&rsquo;s got some work to do, gentlemen, that &rsquo;ll keep him till
      twelve or one; but he&rsquo;ll be down again then, or earlier if he can, and
      hopes some of you&rsquo;ll be left to take a drink with him.&rdquo;

&ldquo;I&rsquo;ve got him!&rdquo; muttered the boy. He continued to himself, looking after
      the shadowy form: &ldquo;Good-by&mdash;good-by for good, Flint Buckner; you
      called my mother a&mdash;well, never mind what; it&rsquo;s all right, now;
      you&rsquo;re taking your last walk, friend.&rdquo;

He brought Sherlock Holmes to the billiard-room, which was jammed with
      eager and admiring miners; the guest called the drinks, and the fun began.
      Everybody was happy; everybody was complimentary; the ice was soon broken;
      songs, anecdotes, and more drinks followed, and the pregnant minutes flew.
      At six minutes to one, when the jollity was at its highest&mdash;

&ldquo;Right you are, Wells-Fargo! It&rsquo;s the grandest luck a new camp ever
      struck. Say, did you see him collar them little rags and dirt and things?
      What an eye! He just can&rsquo;t overlook a clue&mdash;&rsquo;tain&rsquo;t in him.&rdquo;

&ldquo;Sure&rsquo;s you&rsquo;re born! Them odds and ends have got their little old secret,
      and they think there ain&rsquo;t anybody can pull it; but, land! when he sets
      his grip there they&rsquo;ve got to squeal, and don&rsquo;t you forget it.&rdquo;

&ldquo;Boys, I ain&rsquo;t sorry, now, that he wasn&rsquo;t here to roust out the child;
      this is a bigger thing, by a long sight. Yes, sir, and more tangled up and
      scientific and intellectual.&rdquo;

&ldquo;The lad Jones&rsquo;s somewhat distant connection with the case&rdquo; (laughter)
      &ldquo;having been disposed of, let us now call the eye-witnesses of the
      tragedy, and listen to what they have to say.&rdquo;

&ldquo;By Jackson, that&rsquo;s so!&rdquo; said Ham Sandwich, bringing his great hand down
      with a resounding slap upon his thigh; &ldquo;blamed if I ever thought of it
      before.&rdquo;

&ldquo;Nor I!&rdquo; &ldquo;Nor I!&rdquo; cried several. &ldquo;Oh, there can&rsquo;t anything escape
      him&mdash;look
      at his eye!&rdquo;

&ldquo;Gentlemen, distant as the murderer was from his doomed victim, he did not
      wholly escape injury. This fragment of wood which I now exhibit to you
      struck him. It drew blood. Wherever he is, he bears the telltale mark. I
      picked it up where he stood when he fired the fatal train.&rdquo; He looked out
      over the house from his high perch, and his countenance began to darken;
      he slowly raised his hand, and pointed&mdash;

Hillyer broke out with another appeal. &ldquo;Oh, Archy, don&rsquo;t let them do it;
      it would kill mother! You know how I got the hurt. Tell them, and save me,
      Archy; save me!&rdquo;

&ldquo;Yes, I&rsquo;ll save you. Don&rsquo;t be afraid.&rdquo; Then he said to the house, &ldquo;Never
      mind how he got the hurt; it hasn&rsquo;t anything to do with this case, and
      isn&rsquo;t of any consequence.&rdquo;

A comfortable laugh rippled over the house; friend nodded to friend, as
      much as to say, &ldquo;That&rsquo;s the word, with the bark on it. Good lad, good boy.
      He ain&rsquo;t lowering his flag any!&rdquo;

&ldquo;I know about detectives, on account of having them in the family; and if
      you don&rsquo;t want them to find out about a thing, it&rsquo;s best to have them
      around when you do it.&rdquo;

&ldquo;They hung him in San Bernardino last week,&rdquo; added Ferguson, clinching the
      matter, &ldquo;whilst he was searching around after you. Mistook him for another
      man. They&rsquo;re sorry, but they can&rsquo;t help it now.&rdquo;

They all agreed, and whispered and jibbered with joy. But my heart broke;
      for I had read about that man, and knew what it would be to have him upon
      my track, with his superhuman penetration and tireless energies.

&ldquo;Hunt your holes, you scum!&rdquo; which they did; then he said: &ldquo;Follow me,
      Shadbelly; I&rsquo;ll take care of your case myself. No&mdash;keep your pop-gun;
      whenever I see the day that I&rsquo;ll be afraid to have you behind me with that
      thing, it &rsquo;ll be time for me to join last year&rsquo;s hundred and eighty-two&rdquo;;
      and he rode off in a walk, Shadbelly following.
